ency               formance, several limitations remain. CODA is trained\n(time in seconds from audio resumption to the first cor-                 and evaluated exclusively on solo piano from the MSMD\nrect system prediction), and post-jump tracking accuracy                 dataset [24]. Larger datasets that pair real recordings with\n(≤ 1.0 s threshold in the 5 s window after each jump).                   scanned scores would benefit not only this work but the\n    Table 2 shows the results. CYOLO-SB yields low re-                   field as a whole. Extending CODA to other solo instru-\ncovery across both subsets, while CODA without break                     ments is a natural first step. Beyond solo, chamber mu-\nmode struggles because temporal penalties resist large                   sic and orchestral settings pose additional challenges due\njumps. Full CODA reaches .78 at 1 s on repeat versus .64                 to timbral overlap, denser layouts, and multiple simultane-\non random. Annotated repeats resume at musically defined                 ous parts. Finally, evaluation under real-world conditions,\nbar boundaries, while random jumps may resume at arbi-                   including scanned pages, commercial recordings, and live\ntrary onsets and therefore form a harder stress test.                    microphone input, is needed to further assess the method.\n\f Proceedings of the 27th ISMIR Conference, Abu Dhabi, UAE, November 08–12, 2026\n\n               6. ACKNOWLEDGMENTS                               [11] A. Morsi and X. Serra, “Bottlenecks and solutions for\n                                                                     audio to score alignment research,” in Proceedings of\n This work was supported by the Natural Sciences and En-\n                                                                     the 23rd International Society for Music Information\n gineering Research Council of Canada (NSERC; project\n                                         